X86 x86-release-tid-fixed
(* Same protocol and same condition as x86-release-tid-main; the only change is
   the order of P0's two stores, which is the order the fix ships.

   Clearing the tag BEFORE publishing the slot free is what makes the tag safe
   to write with a plain store. P0's clear precedes its unpublish in the total
   store order; P1's claim RMW cannot succeed until that unpublish is visible,
   so it is already ordered after the clear; and P1's tag store cannot hoist
   above its own locked RMW. Per-location coherence then leaves P1's tag last.

   Note this holds on x86 without any fence: the ordering comes from the
   pairing, not from the release store, which is a plain MOV here. The
   Volatile.Write in the C# is doing work only on weaker models -- see
   arm64-release-plainstore for the ARM counterpart. What the two rows here
   establish is that the store ORDER is load-bearing on x86 all by itself. *)
{ lce=0; tid=0; }
 P0            | P1                ;
 MOV [tid],$0  | MOV EDX,$1        ;
 MOV [lce],$0  | XCHG [lce],EDX    ;
               | MOV [tid],$2      ;
exists (lce=1 /\ tid=0)
